* {{rhymes|en|ɛt|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|++}}

# To [[feel]] [[sorry]] about (a thing that has or has not happened), [[afterthink]]: to [[wish]] that a thing had [[not]] [[happen]]ed, that something else had happened instead.
#: {{syn|en|rue}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''regretted''' his words.}}
#: {{ux|en|I don't '''regret''' enrolling in law, but I '''regret''' not studying some Chinese.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=4|passage=Judge Short had gone to town, and Farrar was off for a three days' cruise up the lake. I was bitterly '''regretting''' I had not gone with him when the distant notes of a coach horn reached my ear, and I descried a four-in-hand winding its way up the inn road from the direction of Mohair.}}
#* {{quote-video game|en|developer=Bungie|authorlink=Bungie|title=w:Halo 2|publisher=w:Microsoft Game Studios|date=9 November 2004|system=[[w:Xbox (console)|Xbox]]|role={{w|List of Halo characters#Avery Johnson|Avery Johnson}}|actor=David Scully|level=Outskirts
|passage=Dear humanity, we '''regret''' bein' alien bastards, we '''regret''' comin' to Earth, and we most definitely '''regret''' the Corps just blew up our raggedy-ass fleet!}}
# {{lb|en|more generally}} To feel sorry about (any thing).
#: {{syn|en|grieve|lament|Thesaurus:be sad}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''regret''' that I have to do this, but I don't have a choice.}}
#: {{ux|en|They said they '''regret'''ted to inform us that the train would be late.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|transitive}} To [[miss]]; to feel the loss or absence of; to mourn.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1845|title=The Church of England Magazine|volume=19|page=301
|passage=He more than ever '''regretted''' his home, and with increased desire longed to see his family.}}
#* {{RQ:Stevenson Kidnapped|passage=This [the Cage] was but one of Cluny’s hiding-places; he had caves, besides, and underground chambers in several parts of his country; and following the reports of his scouts, he moved from one to another as the soldiers drew near or moved away. By this manner of living, and thanks to the affection of his clan, he had not only stayed all this time in safety, while so many others had fled or been taken and slain: but stayed four or five years longer, and only went to France at last by the express command of his master. There he soon died; and it is strange to reflect that he may have '''regretted''' his Cage upon Ben Alder.}}

====Usage notes====
* "Regret" is a [[catenative verb]] that takes the [[gerund]] (the {{m|en|-ing}} form), except in set phrases with [[tell]], [[say]], and [[inform]], where the '''to infinitive''' is used. See [[Appendix:English catenative verbs]]

