# Any extended [[protuberance]]; a projecting line or strip.
#: {{antonyms|en|groove}}
#: {{ux|en|The plough threw up '''ridges''' of earth between the furrows.}}
# The [[line]] along which two [[sloping]] [[surface]]s [[meet]] which [[diverge]] towards the ground.
#: {{ux|en|mountain '''ridge'''}}
#* {{RQ:Jefferies Amateur Poacher|chapter=1|passage=It was not far from the house; but the ground sank into a depression there, and the '''ridge''' of it behind shut out everything except just the roof of the tallest hayrick.}}
# The highest point on a roof, represented by a [[horizontal]] line where two roof areas [[intersect]], running the length of the area.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1869|author={{w|Alfred Russel Wallace}}|title={{w|The Malay Archipelago}}|publisher=Macmillan and Co.|year_published=|volume=II|location=London|page=258|passage=Among the domestic animals which may generally be seen in native houses, are gaudy parrots, green, red, and blue, a few domestic fowls, which have baskets hung for them to lay in under the eaves, and who sleep on the '''ridge''', and several half-starved wolfish-looking dogs.}}
#* {{RQ:Bindloss Dust of Conflict|chapter=26|passage=Maccario, it was evident, did not care to take the risk of blundering upon a picket, and a man led them by twisting paths until at last the hacienda rose blackly before them. Appleby could see it dimly, a blur of shadowy buildings with the '''ridge''' of roof parapet alone cutting hard and sharp against the clearing sky.}}
# {{lb|en|fortifications}} The highest portion of the [[glacis]] proceeding from the salient angle of the covered way.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1853-1855|author=w:Joachim Hayward Stocqueler|title=The Life of Field-Marshal the Duke of Wellington|text=the British Guards lie down behind a '''ridge''' to avoid the shot and shell from the opposite heights}}
# A chain of [[mountains]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|I|i|lines=62–66|text={{...}}Which to maintaine, I would allow him oddes, / And meete him, were I tide to runne afoote, / Euen to the frozen '''ridges''' of the Alpes, / Or any other ground [[inhabitable#Etymology_2|inhabitable]], / Where euer Engliſhman durſt ſet his foote.}}
# A chain of [[hills]].
